    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d153">

                <pb facs="0155" n="145" sameAs="frus:frus1944v04/pg145"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 EAC/88: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-02-15T18:00:00+01:00">February 15,
                            1944—6 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received February 16—6:08 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1268. Cornea 28. Department’s 853, February 3, midnight (Eacom 3).<anchor n="19"
                        xml:id="d153-f1"/> I have inquired in writing of my British and Russian
                    colleagues in the Commission whether they would be prepared to undertake at an
                    early meeting of the Commission a discussion of proposals for terms of surrender
                    to be imposed upon Rumania. Neither has yet replied formally. However, I
                    recently had a personal conversation with Gousev<anchor n="20" xml:id="d153-f2"
                    /> in which he told me that he had received instructions about German surrender
                    terms and will be able to join in the discussion of this question at our meeting
                    on Friday next. He also said that he would probably be able to consider Rumanian
                    terms.</p>
                <p>The British reply on the subject will also probably be affirmative. I am told
                    informally, however, that the British are definitely opposed to any proviso that
                    withdrawal of Rumanian forces from certain areas be without prejudice to the
                    ultimate settlement of disputed territorial claims. At a time when they are
                    pressing for an immediate direct settlement of Polish boundary questions, they
                    do not wish to imply that such settlements must await a general peace
                    settlement; and they would ask that this subject not be raised in any way in
                    connection with Rumanian surrender terms.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
